    "text": "To find the minimum value of the function \\( f(x) = x(x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 3) \\), we can start by rewriting the expression in a more convenient form. Notice that:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = x(x + 3)(x + 1)(x + 2) = (x^2 + 3x)(x^2 + 3x + 2)\n\\]\n\nLet \\( y = x^2 + 3x \\). Then the function can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\nf(x) = y(y + 2) = y^2 + 2y\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the minimum value of \\( y^2 + 2y \\) where \\( y = x^2 + 3x \\). To do this, we first find the vertex of the parabola \\( y^2 + 2y \\). The vertex form of a parabola \\( ay^2 + by + c \\) occurs at \\( y = -\\frac{b}{2a} \\). Here, \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 2 \\), so the vertex is at:\n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{2}{2 \\cdot 1} = -1\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( y = -1 \\) into the expression \\( y^2 + 2y \\):\n\n\\[\ny^2 + 2y = (-1)^2 + 2(-1) = 1 - 2 = -1\n\\]\n\nSo, the minimum value of \\( y^2 + 2y \\) is \\(-1\\). Now, we need to check if this value can actually be achieved. We set \\( y = x^2 + 3x = -1 \\) and solve for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nUsing the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\) with \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions are \\( x = \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{-3 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\). Substituting these values back into the original function \\( f(x) \\), we get:\n\n\\[\nf\\left( \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\right) \\left( \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} + 1 \\right) \\left( \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} + 2 \\right) \\left( \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} + 3 \\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying each term:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} + 1 = \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5} + 2}{2} = \\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} + 2 = \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5} + 4}{2} = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} + 3 = \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5} + 6}{2} = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\n\\[\nf\\left( \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\right) \\left( \\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\right) \\left( \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\right) \\left( \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nEach pair of terms in the product is of the form \\( (a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{-3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\right) \\left( \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{5}}",
